  "text": "A prior study introduced a broad framework for describing the realistic stress region of solid materials. In the current work, the bell‑shaped stress regions of several metallic specimens are examined, a new measure called \"plastic deformation efficiency\" is defined, and the path that deformation follows is linked to the stress‑region theory. Strength is taken to be the volume of the closed stress region, because this volume captures the ultimate fracture resistance and the maximum strain‑hardened elastic response for any combination of stresses. The efficiency metric is the ratio of energy spent on permanent shape change to the total of elastic and plastic strain energies; it can be expressed using the octahedral shear strain and a parameter that compares average normal stress to octahedral shear stress. This efficiency grows with strain but drops as hydrostatic pressure rises, and it is reflected in the length and angle of the stress‑state vector relative to the hydrostatic axis. By locating the deformation path relative to the limiting surfaces of the stress region, the study shows, for example, the trajectory during strip‑rolling, and notes a natural tendency for continuous deformation to align toward a particular crystallographic direction that minimizes total strain energy."
